From 94710c5406ff17fd220fd9917b5865445f9a771e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 06:40:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] docs: back up the SQLite database with an online snapshot

The hot backup in docs/operations/backup-restore.md ran
"PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE)" and then copied the main database
file. The control plane keeps committing in between and during the
copy, and an automatic checkpoint rewrites the main file while it is
being copied, so the backup can be inconsistent or malformed. The
script also relied on the exit status of PRAGMA integrity_check,
which is 0 even when the check reports corruption.

The backup script now takes a VACUUM INTO snapshot, fsyncs it,
compares the integrity check output with "ok" and refuses to run
when the database path does not exist (sqlite3 would create an empty
database and back that up). The Python example uses
Connection.backup instead of shutil.copy2: closing another
descriptor of the database file inside the control plane process
would drop the POSIX locks of its SQLite connections.

The guide listed data/registry.db, audit.db and decisions.db, but the
control plane keeps its state in the single FORGEMESH_DB_PATH file
and never opens the audit or decision record stores. Restoring now
keeps the -wal and -shm files next to the renamed old database, since
the -wal can hold committed transactions, and the full restore
example stops the control plane before replacing the file.
